-- `x.f` calls a method of `t.T` on a value of `y.Y`, and `x` does not
-- import `y`, which holds the impl. A `Y` is there only once a module
-- that imports `y` made it (`w`: `y` is complete by then), or `y`
-- itself did in a `let` of its own: then the function of another
-- module reaches every impl `y` writes for traits it does not declare,
-- and the `let` the impls read is set first. The same for `Display`.
import w
import x
import y

fn main() {
  println(y.own)
  println(y.own_shown)
  println(w.early)
  println(w.early_shown)
  println(x.f(y.Y { n: 2 }))
}
